meta {
  name: Broadcasting Auth
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/v1/broadcasting/auth
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{token}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Accept: application/json
  X-Locale: {{locale}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "socket_id": "123456.7890123",
    "channel_name": "private-client.1"
  }
}

docs {
  # Broadcast Channel Auth (Reverb)

  Private-channel authorization for the mobile app. Flutter points its Echo
  `authEndpoint` at this route and passes the same Sanctum Bearer token; the
  guard config (`web` + `sanctum`) lets `Broadcast::auth()` resolve both
  session-based admin users and token-based Client models.

  `socket_id` comes from the live Reverb connection — the hardcoded value here
  only exercises the auth path, a real subscription needs the real id.

  ## Channels

  | Channel                    | Who passes                                        |
  |----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
  | `private-client.{id}`      | the client that owns the account                  |
  | `private-master.{id}`      | the account whose master profile has that id      |

  **One connection covers both.** Since a master profile hangs off the same
  client account and there is only one token, the app opens a single WebSocket
  and subscribes to both channels — no second login, no second socket.

  **Response 200:**
  ```json
  { "auth": "app-key:signature..." }
  ```

  **Errors:**
  - `403` — the token does not own that channel
  - `401` — missing / invalid token
}
